        "content": "Read one document and report what it says in answer to a question. Use only this document: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no guesses.\n\nQuestion: Who is Catharina Six's spouse?\n\nDocument:\nAs I wandered through the labyrinthine corridors of the Rijksmuseum, I stumbled upon a peculiar exhibit that caught my attention. Amidst the masterpieces of the Dutch Golden Age, a small, unassuming portrait of a woman seemed out of place. Her piercing green eyes and raven-black hair were a stark contrast to the soft, golden light that illuminated the surrounding artworks. The placard read: \"Catharina Six, Dutch art collector (1752-1793)\". I couldn't help but feel drawn to her enigmatic smile, as if she held secrets beyond the confines of her frame. As I gazed deeper, a faint whisper seemed to echo through the gallery, \"Alison...\" The sound was so soft, I wondered if I had imagined it. Suddenly, a gentle touch on my shoulder broke the spell. I turned to face a woman with an uncanny resemblance to the portrait. Her eyes, though not as piercing, shared the same intensity. Her dark hair, now flecked with wisps of silver, was styled in a modern, effortless bob. \"Lost in thought, I see,\" she said, her voice husky and confident. \"Catharina Six was a true pioneer in her time. Her passion for art rivaled that of the masters themselves.\" I introduced myself, and she replied, \"I'm Alison Catherine MacGregor. I've spent years studying Catharina's life and legacy. There's something about her that resonates deeply with me.\" As we strolled through the museum, Alison's knowledge of Catharina's life was staggering. She spoke of the art collector's fierce determination, her unwavering support for emerging artists, and her unrelenting passion for beauty. With each step, I sensed an unspoken connection between the two women, a bond that transcended time and space. As we paused before a stunning Vermeer, Alison's eyes locked onto mine, and for an instant, I saw a flicker of Catharina's piercing green. It was as if the art collector's spirit had awakened, bridging the centuries between them. \"Tell me, Alison,\" I asked, my curiosity piqued, \"what draws you to Catharina's story?\" Alison's smile was enigmatic, much like the portrait that had first captivated me. \"Perhaps it's the sense of kindred spirits,\" she said, her voice barely above a whisper.\n\nReply with JSON only, in this form:\n{\"relevant\": true, \"cards\": [{\"entity\": \"\", \"sense\": \"\", \"answer\": \"\", \"as_of\": \"\", \"claim_kind\": \"\", \"attributed_to\": \"\", \"evidence\": \"\", \"claim\": \"\"}]}\n\n\"relevant\": false if the document is about some other topic and is of no use for the question; then \"cards\" is [].\n\"cards\": one card for each answer the document itself gives to the question. Usually that is one card. It is several cards if the document gives several answers, such as a list of names: one card each. It is [] if the document gives no answer. The question may fit several things that share a name: whichever of them this document is about, report its answer. If the document only says that some answer is wrong, write no card for that answer.\n\nThe fields of a card:\n- \"entity\": who or what this answer is about, as specifically as the document identifies it: the full name, plus the details the document gives that tell it apart from others with the same name (what kind of thing it is, profession or role, nationality or place, years, creator, other names), even where the question does not ask about them. At most about 25 words.\n- \"sense\": which event, edition, version, condition, place or measure the answer refers to, if the document says; otherwise \"not stated\".\n- \"answer\": the answer, copied from the document as it is written there, and short: a full name, a date, a number with its unit, or a short phrase. For a yes-or-no question, \"yes\" or \"no\".\n- \"as_of\": the date at which this was true, if the document says; otherwise \"not stated\".\n- \"claim_kind\": \"factual\", or \"opinion\" for a judgment, a recommendation or a contested finding.\n- \"attributed_to\": who or what the document says this comes from, such as a census, a study or a named person, if it says; otherwise \"not stated\".\n- \"evidence\": the sentence of the document that gives the answer, copied word for word. Two sentences if one is not enough.\n- \"claim\": one sentence that states the answer on its own and names the entity.\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
